**`styledText`** (583-604) — full body, shared by both row types, this is where the actual wrapping happens:
```swift
@ViewBuilder private func styledText(_ line: UnifiedDiff.Line) -> some View {
  let highlighted: AttributedString? =
    line.kind == .deletion
    ? line.oldLine.flatMap { highlightedOldLines[$0] }
    : line.newLine.flatMap { highlightedLines[$0] }
  let emphasized: AttributedString? = highlighted == nil ? emphasizedLine(line) : nil
  Group {
    if let highlighted {
      Text(highlighted)
    } else if let emphasized {
      Text(emphasized)
    } else {
      Text(line.text.isEmpty ? " " : line.text).foregroundStyle(codeTextColor)
    }
  }
  .font(.system(.callout, design: .monospaced))
  .textSelection(.enabled)
  .fixedSize(horizontal: false, vertical: true)  // wrap long lines, never truncate
}
```
This `.fixedSize(horizontal: false, vertical: true)` is exactly the modifier the fix plan wants to replace with a pre-measured `.frame(height:)`. Font is `.system(.callout, design: .monospaced)` — see section 3. `.textSelection(.enabled)` is present and must be preserved by any replacement (an `NSAttributedString`/Core Text measurement path must not lose text selection, which is native to SwiftUI `Text`).

**How `highlightedLines`/`highlightedOldLines` apply**: `styledText` looks up the AttributedString by line number (new-side lookup for context/addition, old-side lookup for deletion) and renders it directly via `Text(highlighted)` if present — the coloring/foreground/background runs already live inside the `AttributedString` (built by `DiffHighlightMapper.attributedLines`, not shown in DiffViewer.swift itself). If no highlight is available, falls back to `emphasizedLine(line)` (616-631) which builds a plain-foreground `AttributedString` with a `bg` color behind just the intra-line-emphasis byte range (via `emphasis.deletions`/`emphasis.additions`), or finally plain `Text(line.text)` with `codeTextColor` (636, `Color(nsColor: theme.tokens.nsFg)`).

**`emphasis` effect on rendering**: computed once in `load()` from `IntraLineDiff.emphasis(for: diff)`. Used only inside `emphasizedLine` when no syntax highlight is present, to draw a deeper background tint (`diffAddEmphasisBg`/`diffRemoveEmphasisBg`) behind the changed byte range within an otherwise plain-colored line (`emphasizedPlain`, 639-656, a pure static byte-slicing helper into three `AttributedString` segments: prefix/emphasized/suffix). Note: **`emphasis` does not affect text length or wrapping** — it only changes background color runs, so it's irrelevant to height measurement, but the highlighted `AttributedString` variant could theoretically differ in visual metrics (it doesn't change character content either, only color attributes) — so for measurement purposes the plain `line.text` string is sufficient; attributes (color) never change layout/wrap points, only the *font* (always `.callout` monospaced here — no bold/italic variation observed anywhere in `styledText`).

**Gutter/marker widths** (constants used in row height reasoning, though these don't affect height): `gutterWidth = 88` (521), `sideGutterWidth = 44` (659), marker `.frame(width: 16)` (530).

**`fileHeader`** (168-205): a fixed-height `HStack` (change-letter badge, path text, addition/removal counts, optional `DiffModeSwitch`), `.padding(.horizontal, 10).padding(.vertical, 5)`, `.background(theme.tokens.panel)` — single-line, no wrapping, irrelevant to the row-height fix.

## 2. `UnifiedDiff` model — `macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/UnifiedDiff.swift`

- **`UnifiedDiff`** (line 5): `struct UnifiedDiff: Equatable, Sendable { var hunks: [Hunk]; var truncated: Bool; var renamedFrom: String? }`.
- **`Hunk`** (12-16): `{ var header: String; var lines: [Line] }`.
- **`Line`** (18-32): `{ enum Kind { case context, addition, deletion }; var kind: Kind; var text: String (no marker, no trailing newline); var oldLine: Int?; var newLine: Int? }`.
- **`SideBySideRow`** (178-181, in an `extension UnifiedDiff`): `{ var left: Line?; var right: Line? }`.
- **`UnifiedDiff.parse(_:lineCap:)`** (41-160): default `lineCap: Int = 2000` (line 41 signature) — confirms the 2000-line cap referenced in your description. Parses line-by-line; skips `diff --git`/`index`/mode/`---`/`+++` headers (flushing the current hunk), captures `rename from` into `renamedFrom`, tracks `oldLineNo`/`newLineNo` from each `@@ -a,b +c,d @@` header via regex, drops `\ No newline at end of file` sentinels. **Truncation logic** (122, 152-154): once `totalLines >= lineCap` inside the loop, `truncated = true` is set and a `if truncated { continue }` guard (line 122, checked before line-kind dispatch) causes every subsequent hunk-content line to be skipped — but hunk *headers*/file-headers are still processed after truncation kicks in (so a truncated diff can still start a new empty-lines hunk header if one appears after the cap, though `currentLines` stays empty for it since content is skipped). `flushHunk()` (55-60) appends `Hunk(header:lines:)` and resets. Final `flushHunk()` at 157 catches a trailing unflushed hunk.
- **`UnifiedDiff.isBinary(_:)`** (165-172): sentinel-line detector for `Binary files … differ` / `GIT binary patch`.
- **`UnifiedDiff.sideBySideRows(for:)`** (193-217): iterates a hunk's lines, buffering deletion/addition runs (`dels`/`adds` arrays), flushing via `DiffRunPairing.align(deletions:additions:)` (in `Core/SyntaxHighlighting/DiffRunPairing.swift`) whenever a context line is hit (or at hunk end) — deletion *k* pairs with addition *k*, uneven runs pad the shorter side with `nil`. Context lines emit `SideBySideRow(left: line, right: line)` directly. Pure function, this is the exact per-hunk pairing invoked once in `DiffViewer.load()` at line 279 and stored in the boxed `sbsRows`.

`DiffRunPairing.align` (`Core/SyntaxHighlighting/DiffRunPairing.swift`, 24-31): pads `[DiffRunPair]` to `max(deletions.count, additions.count)` slots, `nil`-padding the shorter side. Shared with `IntraLineDiff.emphasis` (`Core/SyntaxHighlighting/IntraLineDiff.swift`) so the two features (side-by-side layout, intra-line emphasis) never disagree on which deletion pairs with which addition.

## 3. Font/theme used for diff text

**No dedicated diff font token exists in `ThemeTokens`/`ThemeService`.** `ThemeTokens.swift` (`Core/ThemeTokens.swift`) only holds **color** tokens for diffs: `diffAddFg`, `diffRemoveFg`, `nsDiffAddFg`, `nsDiffRemoveFg` (NSColor forms, kept specifically to avoid a lossy `Color`→`NSColor` round-trip — see the comment at lines 58-61), `diffHunkFg`, `diffAddBg`, `diffRemoveBg`, `diffHunkBg`, `diffAddEmphasisBg`, `diffRemoveEmphasisBg` (lines 55-71, computed at lines 152-164 from the theme's palette green/red + accent-or-palette[6], alpha-blended for backgrounds). There is no `diffFont`/`codeFont` token anywhere.

The font is instead hardcoded per call site in `DiffViewer.swift` as a SwiftUI **Dynamic-Type** style, not a fixed point size:
- Line content (`styledText`, line 601) and the `+`/`-` marker (line 528): `.font(.system(.callout, design: .monospaced))`.
- Gutter line numbers (`unifiedGutterNumber`, line 563): `.font(.system(.caption2, design: .monospaced))`.
- Hunk header (`hunkHeader`, line 512) and file-header path/stats (lines 178, 193): `.font(.system(.caption, design: .monospaced))`.

This same `.system(.callout, design: .monospaced)` pattern for code text recurs elsewhere (`ChangesPanel.swift:478`, `ScriptLogContent.swift:71`, `HistoryPanel.swift:496`), so it's the app's de facto "code font," but it is a **Dynamic Type text style** (`.callout`), which resolves to different point sizes depending on the user's accessibility text-size setting — there is **no existing code path anywhere in the app that resolves `.callout` to a concrete `NSFont`/point size** (grepped for `preferredFont`/`TextStyle`/`pointSize` app-wide; only unrelated hit is `GhosttySurfaceView.swift:1929`, an SF Symbol config). This is a real gap for the planned fix: measuring wrapped-text height via `NSAttributedString`/`NSTextContainer` needs a concrete `NSFont`, and `.callout` design `.monospaced` has no direct AppKit equivalent already in use in this codebase — you'd need `NSFont.preferredFont(forTextStyle: .callout, options:)` (a real macOS 10.11+ API, unused here) combined with `NSFont.monospacedSystemFont(ofSize: thatPointSize, weight: .regular)`, or hardcode a fixed point size the way `PlainFileViewer.swift:62` (`NSFont.monospacedSystemFont(ofSize: 12, weight: .regular)`) and `CodeTextView.swift:242` (`numberFont = NSFont.monospacedSystemFont(ofSize: 10, weight: .regular)`) already do elsewhere in the app — those two are the closest existing "fixed-size monospace NSFont" precedents, but neither tracks Dynamic Type, so adopting either exactly would be a font-size behavior change from what `DiffViewer` renders today.

## 4. Existing text-measurement code

Grepped app-wide for `boundingRect`, `NSAttributedString` sizing, `sizeThatFits`, `NSTextStorage`, `CTFramesetter`, `NSLayoutManager`, `lineFragmentRect`, `usedRect`, `size(withAttributes:)`. Two relevant hits, no exact "measure wrapped height for width" utility exists yet — this would be new code — but there are two strong patterns to reuse:

**a) `macapp/WorkroomApp/Views/CodeTextView.swift`** — an `NSViewRepresentable` wrapping a full `NSTextStorage` + `NSLayoutManager` + `NSTextContainer` + `NSTextView` stack (lines 38-63) for the read-only, selectable syntax-highlighted file viewer. This is the closest prior art for "let AppKit do wrapped-text layout instead of guessing":
  - `NSTextContainer(containerSize: NSSize(width: 0, height: .greatestFiniteMagnitude))` with `widthTracksTextView = true` (lines 41-43) — lets the container width follow the view, height unconstrained (grows).
  - `textView.layoutManager?.ensureLayout(for: textView.textContainer!)` (line 117) — forces layout to complete synchronously, which is exactly the primitive a pre-measurement pass would need (call `ensureLayout`, then read line-fragment rects, on an offscreen/throwaway `NSLayoutManager`+`NSTextContainer`+`NSTextStorage` triple sized to the diff's content width — no `NSTextView` or view hierarchy is actually required for pure measurement).
  - `GutterView.draw(_:)` (Views/CodeTextView.swift, 253-296) reads **per-line-fragment rects** via `layoutManager.glyphRange(forBoundingRect:in:)` → `characterRange(forGlyphRange:actualGlyphRange:)` → per line: `layoutManager.glyphIndexForCharacter(at:)` → `layoutManager.lineFragmentRect(forGlyphAt:effectiveRange:)` (line 283) — this is the exact API (`lineFragmentRect`) that would drive a real per-visual-line height sum for a wrapped diff line. `label.size(withAttributes: attrs)` (line 285) is also used there for a single-line number's rendered size via the older `NSString.size(withAttributes:)` sizing API — a second viable (simpler, no container needed) measurement primitive for non-wrapping short strings, though not for wrapped multi-line text.
  - `numberFont = NSFont.monospacedSystemFont(ofSize: 10, weight: .regular)` (line 242) is the existing precedent for a fixed-size monospace `NSFont` constant used purely for AppKit-side drawing/measurement (as opposed to a SwiftUI `Font`).

**b) `macapp/WorkroomApp/Views/InspectorSplitView.swift:432`** — a `sizeThatFits(_:nsViewController:context:)` implementation of the `NSViewControllerRepresentable` protocol requirement, used only to size an entire embedded `NSViewController` to the parent's proposed size (`proposal.replacingUnspecifiedDimensions(by:)`) — this is SwiftUI-representable sizing plumbing, **not** a text-measurement utility; irrelevant to per-line height computation but worth knowing it exists so it isn't confused with the thing you're building.

**No existing usage of `CTFramesetter`/Core Text framesetting** anywhere in the app — if the fix wants a lighter-weight measurement path than a full `NSLayoutManager` stack (e.g., `CTFramesetterSuggestFrameSizeWithConstraints`), that would be new, unprecedented code in this codebase; the `NSTextContainer`+`NSLayoutManager` approach (mirroring `CodeTextView.swift`) has the advantage of matching an established pattern.

## 5. How width changes are currently observed

**Two different idioms coexist in the app**, and `DiffViewer` currently uses the older/simpler one:

- **`DiffViewer.diffBody`** (`Views/DiffViewer.swift:372-378`) uses a bare `GeometryReader { proxy in ... }` and reads `proxy.size.width` **directly inside the view-builder closure** — no `.onChange`/`.onGeometryChange` needed for its own purpose, because SwiftUI re-invokes the closure whenever the reader's size changes, and the closure only needs the width to pick a branch (`showSideBySide(width:)`) each re-render. This is the same idiom used in `Views/WorkroomSplitView.swift:75` (`GeometryReader { geo in let plan = PaneTreeLayout.plan(shown, in: CGRect(origin: .zero, size: geo.size)) ... }`) and `Views/PaneTreeView.swift:49` — both re-derive a full layout plan synchronously from `geo.size` on every reader re-render, no separate change-tracking state. `Views/WorkroomTerminalsView.swift:45` and `Views/ChangesetDetailView.swift:244,251` also use bare `GeometryReader` similarly.

  **However**, this `GeometryReader` in `DiffViewer` wraps only the unified/side-by-side **branch decision** — the measured width is *not* passed down into `unifiedBody`/`sideBySideBody`, `lineRow`, or `styledText`. Any per-line pre-measurement cache keyed by "available content width" would need a *new* width plumb-through: either widen `diffBody`'s `GeometryReader` scope to pass `proxy.size.width` into `unifiedBody(_:width:)`/`sideBySideBody(_:width:)` (and account for `gutterWidth`/`sideGutterWidth`/marker/padding subtracted from it to get the actual text column width per row), or add a second, inner `GeometryReader`/`.onGeometryChange` around just the scrollable content.

- **The newer idiom, `.onGeometryChange(for:of:action:)`** (macOS 14+ API), is used in `Views/OverflowingTabScroller.swift` (lines 109, 151, 233) to push a measured `CGFloat` width into `@State` (e.g. `.onGeometryChange(for: CGFloat.self, of: { $0.size.width }, action: { availableWidth = $0 })`, line 151) — this is the idiom to prefer for a diff-viewer width cache, since a cached per-line-height-by-width table is inherently a "value derived from width changes over time," not a per-render recomputation, and needs an explicit `@State` write to invalidate/rebuild the cache only when the width *actually changes* (not on every body re-render). `WorkroomTabBar.swift:638` and `TerminalTabStrip.swift:596,668` use the older `.background(GeometryReader { geo in Color.clear.preference(key: ..., value: geo.size.width) })` + `.onPreferenceChange` pattern for the same purpose (propagating a per-tab measured width up through a `PreferenceKey`) — a third viable pattern already established in the codebase, though `.onGeometryChange` is more direct or a per-row cache.

**Resize sources that would need to invalidate a width-keyed line-height cache**: window resize, sidebar toggle, inspector resize (`InspectorSplitView.swift`, an `NSSplitViewController`-backed resizable pane), and workroom split-divider drag (`WorkroomSplitView.swift`'s `WorkroomSplitDivider`/`liveRatio`, which live-updates `@State private var liveRatio` on every mouse-moved tick during a drag, throttled to avoid `AppStore`-level republish — note: **that pattern (local `@State` during a live drag, committed once on mouse-up) is itself a template for how the pre-measurement work should behave under a *dragged* divider**: recomputing line heights on every pixel of a live divider drag would be exactly the kind of per-frame CPU cost this fix is trying to avoid elsewhere, so debouncing/deferring the height-cache rebuild until drag-commit (mirroring `liveRatio`'s "local until commit" pattern) is worth considering, rather than reacting to every intermediate `GeometryReader`/`.onGeometryChange` tick during a drag).

